\section{Algorithms}
\emph{Exact moment table.} $M_k(q)=(q^k+q(q-2))/(q(q-1))$ costs $O(\log k)$ operations. A brute-force check costs $O(q^3)$ per $k$.

\emph{Dial check.} For a prime $p$ and exponent $n$, let $g=\gcd(n,p-1)$. The root count of $x^n-2$ is $1$ if $g=1$. Otherwise it is $g$ or $0$, according as $2^{(p-1)/g}\equiv1\pmod p$ or not. This costs $O(\log p)$ multiplications. A count above the degree of the competing polynomial certifies the substitution.

\emph{Sequential discriminator.} Accumulate the running third moment over primes and stop at the first top-letter prime, which is expected after about $20$ primes for $x^5-2$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}
A statistical check of a Galois group can pass only if it was capable of failing. Moments of order $j$ see only the action on $j$-tuples. For the affine family the separating order is exactly $3$, and the complete sequence $M_k(q)=(q^{k-1}+q-2)/(q-1)$ is known in closed form.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Third-moment discriminant.} For a prime $\ell$ and an integer $a$ that is not an $\ell$-th power, the prime-averaged third moment of the root count of $x^\ell-a$ equals $\ell+2$. The group side is Corollary~\ref{cor:three}. What remains is effective convergence rates and large-scale testing.
\item \emph{Moment-truncation blindness.} $k$-transitive groups of equal degree share their first $k$ moments. The pair $\AGL(1,3)$, $\AGL(1,5)$ shows that order $k+1$ can be needed exactly. Characterising which pairs separate at order exactly $k+1$ is open.
\item \emph{Divisor-count second moment.} At a fixed prime, the second moment averaged over constants is within $1/p$ of $\gcd(n,p-1)$. Averaging over primes suggests the value $\tau(n)$, the number of divisors of $n$. We conjecture that for generic $a$ the prime-averaged second moment of the root count of $x^n-a$ is $\tau(n)$.
\end{enumerate}

